          <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker-line"></span> India's former criminal code</p>
          <h1>Indian Penal<br /><em>Code (IPC)</em></h1>
          <p class="catalog-lede">The Indian Penal Code (IPC) set out crimes and punishments in India for many years. This guide explains how it was written, how it was arranged, and what changed when the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) took effect.</p>
          <div class="catalog-meta">
            <span><strong>1834</strong>First Law Commission</span>
            <span><strong>1860</strong>Enacted</span>
            <span><strong>2024</strong>Replaced by BNS</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="origin-sections" aria-label="Indian Penal Code details">
          <article class="origin-detail">
            <span class="info-kicker">01 · Making the code</span>
            <h2>Drafting and enactment</h2>
            <p>The first Indian Law Commission began work on the IPC in 1834. Thomas Babington Macaulay led the Commission. Its goal was to bring criminal laws together in one code.</p>
            <p>The IPC became Act 45 of 1860 and took effect on 1 January 1862. Later laws changed it many times.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="origin-detail">
            <span class="info-kicker">02 · How the code was arranged</span>
            <h2>Organization</h2>
            <p>The IPC grouped laws by topic. Its chapters covered basic rules, exceptions, crimes against people and property, and crimes against the State.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Chapters</strong> grouped related rules together.</li>
              <li><strong>Sections</strong> set out specific rules, including what an offence means and its punishment.</li>
              <li><strong>General exceptions</strong> explained when an act would not count as an offence under the code.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The original code had 23 chapters and 511 numbered sections. Later laws added lettered sections, such as 120A, and removed some sections. This index includes those changes.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="origin-detail">
            <span class="info-kicker">03 · Reach and transition</span>
            <h2>Jurisdiction</h2>
            <p>While it was in force, the IPC generally covered crimes committed in India. It also covered some crimes outside India, including crimes by Indian citizens and crimes on Indian-registered ships or aircraft.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) took effect on 1 July 2024. It replaced the IPC for crimes committed from that date.</li>
              <li>The IPC may still apply to crimes from before that date and to some related cases.</li>
              <li>Courts decide which law applies in each case.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>This page is for learning, not legal advice. Ask a qualified lawyer about a specific case.</p>
          </article>
        </section>

        <section class="ipc-comparison" aria-labelledby="ipc-comparison-title">
          <div class="section-heading ipc-library-heading">
            <div>
              <p class="section-eyebrow">IPC to BNS · Concise comparison</p>
              <h2 id="ipc-comparison-title">What changed?</h2>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ipc-comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="ipc-comparison-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Aspect</th>
                  <th scope="col">IPC, 1860</th>
                  <th scope="col">BNS, 2023</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Enactment and timeline</th>
                  <td>Drafted in 1837 and enacted in 1860; amended over 163 years.</td>
                  <td>Passed on 11 August 2023 and came into force on 1 July 2024.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Origin and purpose</th>
                  <td>Built for British administrative control, uniformity and predictability.</td>
                  <td>Presented as part of legal decolonisation and a sovereign Indian criminal-code framework.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Sections</th>
                  <td>511 sections.</td>
                  <td>358 sections, with related rules merged and outdated provisions removed.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Chapter order</th>
                  <td>Placed offences against the State earlier in the code.</td>
                  <td>Places offences against persons before offences against the State.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Sedition</th>
                  <td>Section 124A punished “exciting disaffection” against the government.</td>
                  <td>Section 152 covers acts endangering sovereignty, unity and integrity; critics argue it is broader.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Organised crime</th>
                  <td>No specific general-code provision; state laws such as MCOCA were used.</td>
                  <td>Section 111 adds organised crime with penalties up to life imprisonment or death.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Terrorism</th>
                  <td>No general IPC provision; separate laws such as UAPA applied.</td>
                  <td>Section 113 creates a terrorism offence in the general penal code.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Sexual offences</th>
                  <td>Sections 375–376D applied, including post-2013 amendments.</td>
                  <td>Sections 63–70 broaden sexual-offence provisions and add Section 69 for false promises of marriage.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Marital rape exemption</th>
                  <td>Non-consensual sex with a wife aged 18 or older was not rape.</td>
                  <td>Exception 2 to Section 63 retains the exemption and remains legally contested.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">New offences</th>
                  <td>Did not contain specific provisions for deceptive sexual relations or organised crime and terrorism.</td>
                  <td>Adds Sections 69, 111, 113 and 117, and stricter hit-and-run provisions.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Community service</th>
                  <td>No community-service punishment.</td>
                  <td>Introduced as an alternative punishment for selected minor offences.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Death penalty offences</th>
                  <td>Several offences carried the death penalty.</td>
                  <td>The death penalty is retained and, in some provisions, expanded.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Legislative process</th>
                  <td>Reform reports proposed major changes, but no full overhaul was enacted promptly.</td>
                  <td>The three criminal-law bills were passed rapidly in 2023 after limited consultation.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Federal and state laws</th>
                  <td>Allowed more room for state amendments and separate state organised-crime statutes.</td>
                  <td>BNS, BNSS and BSA create possible overlap with state amendments and organised-crime laws.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Implementation</th>
                  <td>Police, lawyers and courts had 163 years of familiarity with the code.</td>
                  <td>Full renumbering requires retraining while courts handle pending cases and the transition.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Civil liberties</th>
                  <td>The IPC could be used broadly, with strict bail rules and long pre-trial detention.</td>
                  <td>Broader security offences remain; reforms to bail and speedy-trial safeguards have not resolved the undertrial problem.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Overall assessment</th>
                  <td>Colonial in origin, but adapted to constitutional values by Indian courts over time.</td>
                  <td>A partial and selective update rather than a complete rights-based overhaul.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="ipc-comparison-note">Comparison summary based on “IPC, 1860 vs. BNS, 2023: Complete Comparison Table.” It is provided for general learning, not legal advice.</p>
        </section>

